Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns & how they will be used

• `sample_info.Sample` : will be joined to `variants.call.call_set_name` in order to know which sample generated each genotype call.  
• `sample_info.Super_Population` : super–population code (AFR, AMR, EAS, EUR, SAS …). Used for grouping.  
• `variants.reference_name` : chromosome; we will exclude the sex chromosomes ‘X’ & ‘Y’ and mitochondrial ‘MT’.  
• `variants.start`, `variants.end` : co‑ordinates that keep every variant unique.  
• `variants.AF` (overall) and `variants.AFR_AF`, `AMR_AF`, `ASN_AF`, `EUR_AF` … : allele‑frequency fields that may help to pre‑filter “common” (≥ 0.05) variants.  
• `variants.call` (ARRAY<STRUCT<…>>)  
    – `call.call_set_name` : sample ID.  
    – `call.genotype` (ARRAY<INT64>) : we will check if any element > 0 to decide whether the sample carries an ALT allele.  
• Any query dealing with “common” variants will need the condition “allele frequency ≥ 0.05” in the appropriate AF column (or compute it empirically from the carrier counts).

Typical conditions  
• `WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')` – autosomes only.  
• `WHERE (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0` – sample has at least one ALT allele for that variant.  
• `WHERE v.AF >= 0.05`   – the variant is common in the whole panel (or use super–population specific AF fields).

Below are ten exploratory BigQuery statements, from very simple to close‑to‑final, each capped at 20 rows.

```sql
-- 1.  List all super–population codes and the number of samples in each one.
SELECT 
  s.Super_Population, 
  COUNT(*) AS pop_size
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
GROUP BY s.Super_Population
ORDER BY pop_size DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2.  See which chromosomes exist in the variant table (should include X, Y, MT).
SELECT DISTINCT v.reference_name
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3.  Fetch a few autosomal variants whose overall allele frequency ≥ 0.05.
SELECT 
  v.reference_name, v.start, v.end, v.AF
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND v.AF >= 0.05
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4.  Inspect the call structure for a single variant.
SELECT 
  v.reference_name, v.start, v.end, c.call_set_name, c.genotype
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS c
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5.  Count, for the first 20 variants, how many samples carry an ALT allele (any population).
SELECT 
  v.reference_name, v.start, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT c.call_set_name) AS carrier_count
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS c
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0
GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6.  Join calls to sample_info to know each sample’s super–population.
SELECT 
  c.call_set_name,
  s.Super_Population
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`  AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call)                               AS c
JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
     ON s.Sample = c.call_set_name
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7.  Count carriers PER variant PER super–population (for autosomes).
SELECT 
  v.reference_name,
  v.start,
  s.Super_Population,
  COUNT(DISTINCT c.call_set_name) AS carriers_in_pop
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`  AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call)                               AS c
JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
     ON s.Sample = c.call_set_name
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0
GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, s.Super_Population
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8.  Add each super‑population’s size so that empirical AF can be computed.
WITH pop_sizes AS (
  SELECT Super_Population, COUNT(*) AS pop_size
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
  GROUP BY Super_Population
)
SELECT 
  t.reference_name,
  t.start,
  t.Super_Population,
  t.carriers_in_pop,
  p.pop_size,
  ROUND(t.carriers_in_pop / (2 * p.pop_size), 4) AS empirical_AF
FROM (
  SELECT 
    v.reference_name,
    v.start,
    s.Super_Population,
    COUNT(DISTINCT c.call_set_name) AS carriers_in_pop
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`  AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call)                               AS c
  JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
       ON s.Sample = c.call_set_name
  WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
    AND (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0
  GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, s.Super_Population
) AS t
JOIN pop_sizes AS p
  ON p.Super_Population = t.Super_Population
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9.  Keep only variants that are common (empirical AF ≥ 0.05) in their super‑population
--     and build the “histogram” of (#carriers → #variants) inside each population.
WITH pop_sizes AS (
  SELECT Super_Population, COUNT(*) AS pop_size
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
  GROUP BY Super_Population
), per_variant AS (
  SELECT 
    v.reference_name,
    v.start,
    s.Super_Population,
    COUNT(DISTINCT c.call_set_name) AS carriers_in_pop
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`  AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call)                               AS c
  JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
       ON s.Sample = c.call_set_name
  WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
    AND (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0
  GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, s.Super_Population
)
SELECT 
  pv.Super_Population,
  pv.carriers_in_pop,
  COUNT(*) AS variants_with_that_carrier_count
FROM per_variant AS pv
JOIN pop_sizes AS ps
  ON ps.Super_Population = pv.Super_Population
WHERE pv.carriers_in_pop / (2 * ps.pop_size) >= 0.05
GROUP BY pv.Super_Population, pv.carriers_in_pop
ORDER BY pv.Super_Population, pv.carriers_in_pop
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10.  Final style query: for every super‑population, state the pop size,
--      whether the variant meets AF ≥ 0.05, the number of carriers,
--      and how many variants correspond to that carrier count (histogram).
WITH pop_sizes AS (
  SELECT Super_Population, COUNT(*) AS pop_size
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
  GROUP BY Super_Population
), per_variant AS (
  SELECT 
    v.reference_name,
    v.start,
    s.Super_Population,
    COUNT(DISTINCT c.call_set_name) AS carriers_in_pop
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`  AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call)                               AS c
  JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
       ON s.Sample = c.call_set_name
  WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
    AND (SELECT SUM(g) FROM UNNEST(c.genotype) AS g) > 0
  GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, s.Super_Population
), flagged AS (
  SELECT 
    pv.*,
    ps.pop_size,
    CASE WHEN pv.carriers_in_pop / (2 * ps.pop_size) >= 0.05 THEN TRUE ELSE FALSE END AS is_common
  FROM per_variant AS pv
  JOIN pop_sizes  AS ps
    ON ps.Super_Population = pv.Super_Population
)
SELECT 
  Super_Population,
  pop_size,
  is_common,
  carriers_in_pop,
  COUNT(*) AS variants_count
FROM flagged
GROUP BY Super_Population, pop_size, is_common, carriers_in_pop
ORDER BY Super_Population, carriers_in_pop
LIMIT 20;
```

Each successive statement digs a little deeper, culminating in a result that (when run without the `LIMIT 20`) would satisfy:  

• autosomes only,  
• common variants (AF ≥ 0.05 within the super‑population),  
• the super‑population’s total size,  
• number of samples sharing the variant, and  
• how many such variants there are for that carrier count.
